## 5.7.5Signed integers

The twos’ complement representation performs exactly the same arithmetic as we have seen for unsigned types, but the upper half of unsigned values (those with a high-order bit of ) is interpreted as being negative. The following two functions are basically all that is needed to interpret unsigned values as signed values: Table 5.7 shows an example of how the negative of value 240 can be constructed. For unsigned types, -A can be computed as A + 1 . [Exercise 12] [Exercise 13] [Exercise 14]

```c
bool is_negative(unsigned a) {
  constexpr unsigned int_max = UINT_MAX/2;
  return a > int_max;
}
bool is_signed_less(unsigned a, unsigned b) {
  if (is_negative(a) != is_negative(b)) return a > b;
  else return a < b;
}
```

## 5.7.5Signed integers

When done that way, signed integer arithmetic will again behave more or less nicely. Unfortunately, there is a pitfall that makes the outcome of signed arithmetic difficult to predict: overflow. Where unsigned values are forced to wrap around, the behavior of a signed overflow is undefined C . The following two loops look much the same: We know what happens for the first loop: the counter is incremented up to UINT_MAX and then wraps around to . All of this may take some time, but after UINT_MAX-1 iterations, the loop stops because i will have reached .

```c
for (unsigned i = 1; i; ++i) do_something();
for (  signed i = 1; i; ++i) do_something();
```

