## 19.5Long jumps

Normal syntactical labels are only valid goto targets within the same function as they are declared. In contrast, a jmp_buf is an opaque object that can be declared anywhere and used as long as it is alive and its contents are valid. In descend , we use just one jump target of type jmp_buf , which we declare as a local variable. This jump target is set up in the base function basic_blocks that serves as an interface to descend (see listing 19.2 ). This function mainly consists of one big switch statement that handles all the different conditions. The 0 branch of that switch is taken when we come here through the normal control flow. This is one of the basic principles for setjmp .

```c
void basic_blocks(void) {
  char buffer[maxline];
  unsigned depth = 0;
  char const* format =
    "All matching %0.0d'%c' '%c' pairs have been closed correctly\n";
  jmp_buf jmpTarget;
  switch (setjmp(jmpTarget)) {
  case 0:
    descend(nullptr, &depth, maxline, buffer, jmpTarget);
    break;
  case plusL:
    format =
      "Warning: %d '%c' have not been closed properly (expected '%c')\n";
    break;
  case plusR:
    format =
      "Error: closing too many (%d) '%c' parenthesis with
            additional '%c'\n";
    break;
  case tooDeep:
    format =
      "Error: nesting (%d) of '%c' '%c' constructs is too deep\n";
    break;
  case eofOut:
    format =
      "Error: EOF for stdout at %d open '%c', expecting same amount
            of '%c'\n";
    break;
  case interrupted:
    format =
      "Interrupted at level %d of '%c' '%c' nesting\n";
    break;
  default:;
    format =
      "Error: unknown error within (%d) '%c' '%c' constructs\n";
  }
  fflush(stdout);
  fprintf(stderr, format, depth, left, right);
  if (interrupt) {
    SH_PRINT(stderr, interrupt,
             "is somebody trying to kill us?");
    raise(interrupt);
  }
}
```

