## Union Size and Memory Layout

Here's a C example to illustrate the size and memory layout of a union: In this example, the `Data` union has three members: `i` of type `int`, `c` of type `char`, and `f` of type `float`. The size of the `Data` union will be equal to the size of the largest member, which is `float` in this case. The memory layout will allow each member to occupy the same memory space, resulting in overlapping values when one member is assigned after another.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

union Data {
    int i;
    char c;
    float f;
};

int main() {
    union Data data;

    printf("Size of data: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(data));

    data.i = 10;
    printf("Value of i: %d\n", data.i);

    data.c = 'A';
    printf("Value of c: %c\n", data.c);
    printf("Value of i after assigning c: %d\n", data.i);

    data.f = 3.14;
    printf("Value of f: %f\n", data.f);
    printf("Value of i after assigning f: %d\n", data.i);

    return 0;
}
```

