## Input Handling: Using \`fgets()\`

For reading user input from the console, \`scanf()\` can be problematic with strings containing spaces or buffer overflows. \`fgets()\` is a safer alternative: \`fgets()\` reads up to \`BUFFER\_SIZE - 1\` characters or until a newline, including the newline character itself. \`strcspn()\` can be used to remove this newline for cleaner messaging.

```c
char input_buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
printf("Enter message: ");
fgets(input_buffer, BUFFER_SIZE, stdin);

// Remove trailing newline character if present
input_buffer[strcspn(input_buffer, "\n")] = 0;
```

